   ['After independence, many governments launched Universal Primary Education: for example, Nigeria (UPE, 1976), Kenya (Free Primary Education, 2003) and Uganda (UPE, 1997). Nigeria\'s Universal Basic Education Act (2004) provides free, compulsory nine-year basic education (six years primary, three years junior secondary). Ghana introduced Free Senior High School in 2017. Kenya began moving from the 8-4-4 system to the Competency-Based Curriculum (2-6-3-3-3) in 2017.',
    'Education policy balances access (getting children into school), equity (fair opportunities for girls, rural children, the poor and learners with disabilities) and quality (whether children actually learn). Rapid expansion has often led to large classes, shortages of trained teachers and learning materials, and learning outcomes below expectations.',
    'Globally, Sustainable Development Goal 4 (2015) aims to "ensure inclusive and equitable quality education and promote lifelong learning opportunities for all" by 2030. Funding benchmarks suggest countries spend at least 4 to 6 percent of GDP, or 15 to 20 percent of public spending, on education.'],
   ['Nigeria UBE Act 2004: 9 years free basic education', 'Ghana Free SHS: 2017', 'Kenya CBC: from 2017', 'SDG 4: by 2030; 15-20% of public spending'],
